1. Chosen Development Model
Model: Agile Development Model
Justification: I chose the Agile model because it allows flexibility and continuous improvement during development. Since the Smart Study Planner may require changes based on user feedback (students’ needs), Agile is suitable for iterative development and quick updates,
Agile also helps in:
Dividing the project into small modules (tasks, scheduling, reminders) and frequent testing and improvements
2. User Requirements
a. Stakeholders 
-Students (End Users)
Primary users of the system
Create tasks, set deadlines, and track study progress
-Developers
Responsible for designing, building, and maintaining the system and to ensure system functionality and performance
b. User Stories 
Student User Stories
As a student, I want to create study tasks, so that I can organize my workload.
As a student, I want to set deadlines for tasks, so that I don’t miss important submissions.
As a student, I want to view my daily schedule, so that I can manage my time effectively.
As a student, I want to mark tasks as completed, so that I can track my progress.
Admin Management 
As an admin, I want to manage user accounts, so that the system remains organized and secure.
As an admin, I want to view system activity, so that I can monitor usage.
3. Functional Requirements 
a. Description
The system must provide the following core functionalities: 
-User registration and login
-Task creation, editing, and deletion
-Scheduling study plans
-Deadline reminders
-Task completion tracking
b. Acceptance Criteria 
User Login
-User enters valid email and password
-System verifies credentials
-User is redirected to dashboard after successful login
-Error message shown for invalid login
Task Management
-User can create a new task with title, description, and deadline
-User can edit or delete tasks
-Changes are saved and updated immediately in system
Scheduling System
-User can assign tasks to specific dates
-Tasks appear in calendar or daily view
-System correctly displays scheduled tasks
Task Completion
-User can mark tasks as completed
-Completed tasks are visually separated from pending tasks
4. Non-Functional Requirements 
a. Description
The system should be: fast and responsive, easy to use, reliable and secure and accessible on multiple devices (desktop/laptop/mobile browser)
b. Acceptance Criteria
-Performance
Pages load in less than 2 seconds
Task operations (add/edit/delete) complete in under 3 seconds
-Usability
Interface is simple and user-friendly
New users can understand system within 5 minutes
-Reliability
System uptime should be 99% during usage
No data loss during normal operations
-Responsiveness
Application works on mobile, tablet, and desktop screens
UI adapts to different screen sizes properly
5. Application Specifications
a. Architecture
The system follows a three-tier architecture:
-Frontend: User interface (web pages for login, dashboard, tasks)
-Backend: Handles logic such as task management, authentication, scheduling
-Database: Stores user data, tasks, deadlines, and progress
b. Database Model
The system includes the following tables:
-Users Table
user_id (Primary Key)
name
email (Unique)
password
-Tasks Table
task_id (Primary Key)
user_id (Foreign Key)
title
description
deadline
status (Pending / Completed)
-Relationships:
One user can have many tasks
Each task belongs to one user
c. Technologies Used
The system is developed using PHP for handling logic and system operations
The frontend uses HTML, CSS, and JavaScript for building responsive and interactive UI.
MySQL / Firebase is used as the database for storing structured user and task data.
d. User Interface Design
The interface includes a Login Page (user authentication), a Dashboard (overview of tasks and progress), a Task Management Page (add/edit/delete tasks) and a Calendar View.(optional)
Design Principles:
-Simple and clean layout
-Easy navigation menu
-Clear buttons for actions (Add Task, Edit, Delete)
-Responsive design for all devices
e. Security Measures
The system uses user authentication using secure login system. Passwords are stored in encrypted form. Role-based access control ensures that only admins can manage
system data. (user/admin separation if needed). Input validation to prevent invalid or malicious data. Secure session handling to protect user accounts
